1
00:00:03,667 --> 00:00:08,200
Example A: the lecture's neuron, first as a cartoon of a brain cell.

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,000
Example A: a neuron takes numbers in and gives one number out.

3
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:20,933
Each weight scales its input: a big weight makes that input matter more.

4
00:00:21,533 --> 00:00:28,667
The bias shifts the sum up or down, whatever the inputs say.

5
00:00:29,267 --> 00:00:37,133
The squashing function tanh maps any number into the range minus 1 to 1.

6
00:00:37,733 --> 00:00:44,800
Big inputs flatten toward 1 and big negative inputs toward minus 1.

7
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:51,867
Predict: with bias 8 the sum is n = 2. Is tanh(2) near 2 or near 1?

8
00:00:52,467 --> 00:01:00,733
It is 0.9640: already in the flat tail, so the output barely moves.

9
00:01:01,333 --> 00:01:06,200
Now the same neuron as Value nodes: inputs, weights and a bias of 8.

10
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:18,600
The forward pass fills in each number, column by column, left to right.

11
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:24,400
With bias 8 the sum is 2 and tanh gives 0.9640, as in the plot.

12
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,133
Predict: lower the bias to 6.8814. Does o go up or down from 0.9640?

13
00:01:30,733 --> 00:01:50,333
The sum drops to 0.8814 and tanh leaves the flat tail: o is 0.7071.

14
00:01:50,933 --> 00:01:55,800
tanh is a single node here: it cannot be built from plus and times.

15
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:05,267
Backward pass: start at the output, where o.grad is 1.

16
00:02:05,867 --> 00:02:11,600
Predict: o is 0.7071. What is one minus o squared?

17
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:26,067
The local derivative of tanh is 1 minus o squared.

18
00:02:26,667 --> 00:02:50,000
A plus node is a router: it hands the same gradient to both inputs.

19
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:57,333
Predict: x2 is 0 and the product gets 0.5. What is w2.grad?

20
00:02:57,933 --> 00:03:16,067
A times node swaps in the other input: w2.grad is x2 times 0.5.

21
00:03:16,667 --> 00:03:22,000
w2.grad is 0 because x2 is 0: wiggling w2 changes nothing.

22
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:40,733
Same swap for x1: w1.grad is x1 times 0.5, and x1.grad is w1 times 0.5.

23
00:03:41,333 --> 00:03:45,667
w1.grad is 1.0: to raise this neuron's output, push w1 up.

24
00:03:46,267 --> 00:03:51,467
Example B: what if the bias is 0, or x2 is 1? Watch the gradients.

25
00:03:52,067 --> 00:03:56,600
Same neuron, but now the tanh curve and the four gradients live together.

26
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:01,400
Predict: set the bias to 0. Will the gradients grow or shrink?

27
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:14,000
The sum slides left to n = -6, deep in the flat tail of tanh.

28
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:20,400
The local derivative is almost 0, so every gradient is almost 0.

29
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,533
Now restore the bias and give x2 the value 1 instead of 0.

30
00:04:26,133 --> 00:04:30,067
Predict: x2 goes from 0 to 1. What happens to w2.grad?

31
00:04:30,667 --> 00:04:35,267
The sum rises to 1.8814 and the dot slides right along the curve.

32
00:04:35,867 --> 00:04:42,867
w2.grad wakes up: x2 times the local derivative, 1 times 0.0887.

33
00:04:43,467 --> 00:04:48,367
x2 now sits closer to the tail, so every gradient is smaller than before.

34
00:04:48,967 --> 00:04:53,167
Expert corner: tanh gradients vanish in the tails, and ReLU can die.

35
00:04:53,767 --> 00:04:58,767
Expert corner: tanh and its slope, 1 minus tanh squared, drawn together.

36
00:04:59,367 --> 00:05:03,567
In the red bands the slope is under 0.1: the gradient vanishes.

37
00:05:04,167 --> 00:05:13,067
Predict: far out on the tail, is the slope big or tiny?

38
00:05:13,667 --> 00:05:18,533
At n = 4 the slope is 0.0013: weights feeding this neuron barely learn.

39
00:05:19,133 --> 00:05:24,033
So the scale of weights and inputs matters: huge sums land in the tails.

40
00:05:24,633 --> 00:05:33,213
ReLU keeps positive numbers and zeroes the negatives. Meet two ReLU neurons.

41
00:05:33,813 --> 00:05:39,893
The healthy one passes x times w = 2 straight through.

42
00:05:40,493 --> 00:05:55,873
Predict: now w = -1 and x = 2. Does any gradient get through?

43
00:05:56,473 --> 00:06:02,653
The sum is -2, so relu outputs 0 and blocks the backward flow.

44
00:06:03,253 --> 00:06:09,853
Both gradients are exactly 0.0: this neuron can never recover on its own.

